Two modules share the *identical* interpolated declaration
`background-color: $(Color.Background.Alt.box)` under the same `header`
binding, differing only in one sibling declaration (`display: flex` vs
`padding: 8px`).

Guard for the /dashboard "header renders with no background" bug: a rule
must read the variable that its own element sets. The module is in the
class and in the variable seed, so each module's rule reads its own
variable, and its `CSS.make` sets that variable.

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml a.re > a.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl a.ml -o a.ml
  $ refmt --parse re --print ml b.re > b.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl b.ml -o b.ml

Each module has its own class and variable, and its runtime binding sets
that variable:

  $ refmt --parse ml --print re a.ml
  [@css "@property --2wfidwj{syntax:\"*\";inherits:false;}"];
  [@css ".css-2wfiyu5{background-color:var(--2wfidwj);display:flex;}"];
  [@css.classes [("css-2wfiyu5", 1, "A.header", "a.ml", 2, 11, 2, 73)]];
  [@css.bindings [("A.header", "css-2wfiyu5")]];
  let header =
    CSS.make(
      "css-2wfiyu5",
      [("--2wfidwj", CSS.Types.Color.toString(Color.Background.Alt.box))],
    );
  $ refmt --parse ml --print re b.ml
  [@css "@property --lgcigd9{syntax:\"*\";inherits:false;}"];
  [@css ".css-lgcilvi{background-color:var(--lgcigd9);padding:8px;}"];
  [@css.classes [("css-lgcilvi", 1, "B.header", "b.ml", 2, 11, 2, 72)]];
  [@css.bindings [("B.header", "css-lgcilvi")]];
  let header =
    CSS.make(
      "css-lgcilvi",
      [("--lgcigd9", CSS.Types.Color.toString(Color.Background.Alt.box))],
    );

Aggregating both modules keeps both blocks, each with its own variable:

  $ styled-ppx.generate a.ml b.ml > styles.css
  $ cat styles.css
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @property --2wfidwj{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  .css-2wfiyu5{background-color:var(--2wfidwj);display:flex;}
  @property --lgcigd9{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  .css-lgcilvi{background-color:var(--lgcigd9);padding:8px;}
